Objective: This 21 day email course is designed for the student to gain
a clear understanding of the credit repair process so that they can
approach the client file and create an action plan with certainty. The
content of this course is also designed to help the student effectively
communicate credit information so that as a business owner they can feel
comfortable and confident when speaking about the subject of credit to
leads, clients, and the general public. 
Dispute 21 - Day 13:

The Furnisher Rule: 
The Obligations of Information Furnishers When Reporting to a CRA

Bruce, in the previous lesson we took a look at the meaning of
"Accuracy" and "Integrity" in relation to the reporting standard of
information placed on consumer reports. We also discussed what it means
to be a "furnisher" of information and defined what a direct dispute is.

In today's lesson we will be covering exactly what, when and how a
furnisher of information is required to report to the credit reporting
agencies (CRA).

The Federal Trade Commission's Bureau of Consumer Rights* published a
Furnisher Rule that outlines the duties of furnishers when reporting
information to CRAs and handling disputes about the information they
report. We will be taking a direct look at what this rule entails to
gain an understanding of how Original Creditors, Collection Agencies and
all other companies furnishing information to a reporting agency are
required to respond when handling disputes. 

Remember, when submitting a direct dispute to the furnisher, they are
only required to comply with these obligations if you have followed the
guidelines for submitting a direct dispute discussed in the previous
lesson.
  
*The FTC's Bureau of Consumer Protection stops unfair, deceptive and
fraudulent business practices by collecting complaints and conducting
investigations, suing companies and people that break the law,
developing rules to maintain a fair marketplace, and educating consumers
and businesses about their rights and responsibilities.* 

see FCRA 

The FCRA requires that if a furnisher furnishes any information to a
CRA, they must include any of the following items that are applicable to
the information they are furnishing. 

Credit Limits - Usually, the furnisher must include a consumer's credit
limit.

Disputed Information - Once a consumer disputes information directly
with a furnisher, the furnisher may not report that information to a CRA
without telling the CRA that the information is in dispute. FCRA 623
(a)(3)

Closed Accounts - If a furnisher reports information to a CRA on a
regular basis, the furnisher must notify the CRA that a consumer has
voluntarily closed an account the next time they send information that
would normally include that account. This is important because some
users of information may interpret a closed account as an indicator of
bad credit unless the furnisher clearly discloses that the consumer, not
the creditor, closed the account. FCRA 623(a)(4)

Delinquent Accounts - When a furnisher refers an account for collection
and notifies a CRA that they have done so, they also must report the
date of delinquency to the CRA within 90 days. The date of delinquency
is the month and year the consumer's delinquency resulting in the
referral began. 

If a Debt Collector furnishes information to a CRA about the accounts of
a creditor, they must report the date of delinquency given to them by
the creditor. FCRA 623(a)(5)(A)
The "date of delinquency" determines how long the debt can be reported
on a consumer's credit report. Generally, a CRA may report a delinquent
debt for 7 years from the date of delinquency. If the debt was
discharged in bankruptcy, a CRA may report it for 10 years.
If the creditor did not report the date of delinquency to a collection
agency, they have two (2) options:
1. Establish and follow reasonable procedures to determine the date from
the original creditor or another reliable source.
2. If the Collection Agency cannot determine the date, they may
establish and follow reasonable procedures to ensure that the reported
date of delinquency is a date before the account was referred to
collector or charged off. 
Bruce, the two options above help us to understand why it is
critical to request the date of first delinquency in the debt validation
letter to the collection agency. It also helps us to see why re-aging an
account is illegal. Once a collection agency gets an account, either
from the original creditor or another collection agency, they sometimes
try to illegally change dates to restart the statute of limitations as
well as the reporting period in an attempt to force a consumer to pay
the debt or extend the time period for which they can sue. Please note
that if the date of first delinquency is changed by a collection agency
or any creditor, this will directly affect the credit score in a
negative way because it will appear as if the delinquent or negative
account is newer than it actually is.

Furnisher's Duty to Correct and Update Information

If a furnisher reports information to a CRA on a regular basis and
determines that any information they have provided is inaccurate or
incomplete, they must promptly notify the CRA and provide coreections or
additions. Going forward, they must furnish only the correct information
to the CRA. FCRA 623 (a)(2)(B)

Negative Information from Financial Institutions

If a financial institution extends credit and regularly reports negative
information about their customer to a nationwide CRA (ex: Equifax), they
must notify their customers that they are reporting such negative
information. Examples of negative information include delinquencies,
late payments, or any form of default. FCRA 623(a)(7)(G)(i)

They must provide the notice either before they furnish the negative
information or within 30 days of furnishing it. They may include the
notice wit a notice of default, a billing statement, or another item
sent to the consumer, but they cannot send it with a Truth In Lending
Act notification. The notices must be clear and conspicuous.

Bruce, It is always important to ask your client information
pertaining to their negative accounts and if they have received such
disclosures or notices. This can and should be done during your analysis
appointment. This can be a disputable reason with delinquent accounts
and a violation if the client never received anything notifying them of
the negative information within the 30 day period mentioned above. 

Medical Information

If the primary business of a furnisher is providing medical services,
products, or devices and they, their agent, or an assignee reports
information about consumers to CRAs, they must notify each CRA that they
are a medical provider. FCRA 623(a)(9)

This notice helps the CRAs comply with their FCRA duties with regard to
the reporting of medical information. FCRA 604(g)

For example, if the name, address, and telephone number of a medical
information furnisher appears on a consumer report, the information must
be encoded so it doesn't identify the specific provider or the nature of
the services, products, or devices. FCRA605(a)(6)(A)

People often have questions regarding HIPAA Laws and what can be
reported, how it should be reported, etc. 

When reviewing a client's credit report, if you can infer any of the
specifics in bold type above from the information being reported by a
furnisher regarding medical services then it is highly possible the
account is in violation of HIPPAA.

When requesting Validation from a collection company furnishing medical
debt information, if you can determine any of the specific in above in
bold type from the "validation" documentation you receive, then it is
highly possible the account is in violation of HIPPA.

In tomorrow's lesson, we will cover the duties and procedures of the
Furnisher once they receive your "direct" dispute, so that you are aware
of how your dispute investigation should be handled with the
furnisher.   

